Abstract

We investigate the relationship between the number of distinct ways to arrange a set of \(n\) distinct objects and the factorial function. Specifically, we show that the number of permutations of \(n\) objects is given by \(n!\), and we discuss how this result can be used to compute probabilities in combinatorial problems. We also explore extensions to cases where the objects are not distinct and provide examples of applications in probability theory.

\section{Introduction}

The factorial function, denoted by \(n!\), is a fundamental concept in combinatorics and probability theory. It represents the number of ways to arrange \(n\) distinct objects in a sequence. In this paper, we provide a detailed analysis of the factorial function and its applications in combinatorial problems. We also discuss how the factorial function can be used to compute probabilities in various scenarios, such as rolling dice and drawing cards from a deck.

