\section{Introduction}

The factorial function, denoted by \(n!\), is a fundamental concept in combinatorics and probability theory. It represents the product of all positive integers up to \(n\) and is defined recursively as \(n! = n \times (n-1)!\) with the base case \(0! = 1\). The factorial function plays a crucial role in counting the number of ways to arrange \(n\) distinct items, as well as in the computation of probabilities in discrete sample spaces.

\section{Combinatorial Interpretation}

The factorial function arises naturally in the context of permutations. For a set of \(n\) distinct elements, the number of possible orderings is \(n!\). This combinatorial interpretation is essential for understanding the applications of factorials in counting problems and probability calculations.

